# --- part 1: Vedic and classical India
E+=[
X('rigveda-aryan-debate',"The Rigveda and the 'Aryan' debate: who brought Sanskrit to India?",H,'disputed',2,-1500,['textual (oral tradition)','linguistic','genetic'],
 "The Rigveda, a collection of 1,028 hymns in Vedic Sanskrit, is among the oldest texts in any Indo-European language. Most scholars date its composition to about 1500–1200 BCE in the north-west of the Indian subcontinent (the Punjab). It was handed down orally for more than two thousand years with remarkable precision before being written down; manuscripts of the Rigveda are on UNESCO's Memory of the World register. Because Sanskrit is related to Persian, Greek, Latin and the Germanic languages, most linguists conclude that Indo-Aryan speakers entered South Asia from Central Asia in the 2nd millennium BCE, after the Indus civilisation had declined. A 2019 ancient-DNA study (Narasimhan et al., Science) found steppe-related ancestry arriving in South Asia in the early-to-mid 2nd millennium BCE, which most researchers read as support for this migration.",
 [('Indo-Aryan migration','The mainstream view combines linguistics, the Rigveda\'s own geography and ancient DNA: a gradual movement of steppe-derived pastoralists, not a single violent \'invasion\', mixing with existing populations.'),
  ('Indigenous Aryans / Out of India','A minority of scholars, mostly in India, argue that Vedic culture developed within South Asia, perhaps identical with the Indus civilisation, and that Indo-European languages spread outward from India. They point to the Rigveda\'s praise of a mighty Sarasvati river, which some identify with the now-dry Ghaggar-Hakra. Most linguists and geneticists consider this view inconsistent with the evidence.'),
  ('Astronomical dating','Some writers, from B. G. Tilak (The Arctic Home in the Vedas, 1903) onwards, have read astronomical references in the Vedas as pointing to dates of 4000 BCE or much earlier; these interpretations are not accepted by most Indologists (speculative).'),
  ('Politics of the debate','Since the colonial era, when \'Aryan\' was misused in European race theories, the question has been entangled with nationalism and identity, which both sides accuse the other of letting shape their conclusions.')],
 ['wiki:Rigveda','wiki:Indo-Aryan_migrations','doi:10.1126/science.aat7487'],'Rigveda',dateNote='Composition usually dated c. 1500–1200 BCE'),
X('mahabharata-kurukshetra','The Mahabharata and the Kurukshetra war: a remembered battle or an ancient nuclear war?',M,'myth-legend',2,-1000,['textual','archaeological','mythological'],
 "The Mahabharata, at around 100,000 verses one of the longest poems ever composed, tells of the war at Kurukshetra between two branches of the Kuru royal family, the Pandavas and the Kauravas, and contains the Bhagavad Gita. The text grew over many centuries, roughly 400 BCE to 400 CE, and is traditionally attributed to the sage Vyasa. Tradition places the war at the start of the Kali Yuga, 3102 BCE. Historians who accept a historical kernel usually link it to the Kuru kingdom of the early first millennium BCE (Painted Grey Ware culture), perhaps c. 1000 BCE; others regard the war as essentially literary. The epic describes divine weapons such as the brahmastra, which can destroy whole armies.",
 [('Historical kernel','Many historians think a real conflict among Iron Age Kuru-Panchala clans may lie behind the story, greatly magnified by later poets; there is no direct archaeological evidence of the battle itself.'),
  ('Traditional and astronomical dates','Hindu tradition dates the war to 3102 BCE; various writers have used planetary positions described in the epic to propose dates from c. 5500 to 1500 BCE, with little agreement between them.'),
  ('Ancient nuclear war claim','From the 1960s–70s, writers such as Charles Berlitz and David Davenport claimed that the brahmastra descriptions recall nuclear blasts and that skeletons at Mohenjo-daro show signs of a sudden catastrophe or radiation. Archaeologists note that no unusual radioactivity has been reported, that the skeletons came from different periods, and that George Dales argued in 1964 that the \'massacre\' at Mohenjo-daro was a misreading (speculative).'),
  ('Modern echo','Robert Oppenheimer famously recalled a line of the Bhagavad Gita, \'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds\', after the 1945 Trinity test, which has helped link the epic to nuclear imagery in popular culture.')],
 ['wiki:Kurukshetra_War','wiki:Mahabharata','wiki:Brahmastra'],'Kurukshetra_War',dateNote='Tradition: 3102 BCE; historians who accept a kernel: c. 1000 BCE; text composed c. 400 BCE–400 CE'),
X('ashoka-edicts','The edicts of Ashoka: a forgotten emperor read again after 2,000 years',H,'confirmed',2,-260,['inscriptions','textual','archaeological'],
 "Ashoka, emperor of the Maurya dynasty (r. c. 268–232 BCE), ruled most of the Indian subcontinent. After his bloody conquest of Kalinga (c. 261 BCE) he turned to Buddhism and had edicts carved on rocks and polished stone pillars across his empire, mostly in Prakrit in the Brahmi script, but also in Kharosthi, Greek and Aramaic. They speak of remorse for the war, tolerance between religious groups, care for animals and the sending of envoys to Hellenistic kings. Over the following centuries the Brahmi script was forgotten. In 1837 the British scholar James Prinsep deciphered it, but the king called 'Devanampiya Piyadasi' ('Beloved of the gods, of gracious mien') was only firmly identified with the Ashoka of Buddhist chronicles when an edict naming him was found at Maski in 1915.",
 [('A model ruler?','Buddhist texts such as the Ashokavadana portray a cruel king transformed into an ideal Buddhist monarch who built 84,000 stupas. Historians debate how much of his \'dhamma\' policy was personal conviction and how much was a political tool for holding together a vast, diverse empire.'),
  ('Secret society legend','Talbot Mundy\'s 1923 novel The Nine Unknown claimed that Ashoka founded a secret society of nine men to guard dangerous knowledge; the story is fiction, though it is sometimes repeated as fact.'),
  ('Rediscovery','The rediscovery is often cited as an example of how a major ruler can largely vanish from historical memory and be restored by decipherment; Ashoka\'s lion capital is now India\'s national emblem.')],
 ['wiki:Edicts_of_Ashoka','wiki:Ashoka','wiki:James_Prinsep'],'Edicts_of_Ashoka',end=-232),
X('nalanda','Nalanda: the great Buddhist university and the fire that burned for months',H,'confirmed',2,427,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Nalanda, in Bihar, India, was a large Buddhist monastic centre of learning from about the 5th century CE, under the Gupta kings, to around 1200 CE. Pilgrim-scholars such as Xuanzang (7th century) and Yijing described thousands of monks studying Buddhist philosophy, logic, grammar and medicine. Nalanda declined and was sacked around 1200, usually attributed to troops of the Turkic general Bakhtiyar Khalji, and was abandoned. Its ruins were identified in the 19th century and excavated by the Archaeological Survey of India from 1915; the site became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2016, and a new Nalanda University opened nearby in 2014.",
 [('Destroyed by Khalji','The common account holds that Khalji\'s forces killed the monks and burned the libraries, which were said to have smouldered for months. The nearest contemporary Persian chronicle (Minhaj-i-Siraj) describes the sacking of a fortified monastery that most scholars identify as Odantapuri or Vikramashila, not Nalanda by name.'),
  ('Tibetan accounts','Later Tibetan sources, including Taranatha (17th century), describe further destruction and a fire attributed to hostile non-Buddhist ascetics, and say teaching continued in a reduced way for a time after the raid.'),
  ('Long decline','Some historians stress that Buddhist institutions in India were already declining from loss of royal patronage and competition with Hindu devotional movements, so the raid was the end of a longer process. The story remains politically sensitive in modern India.')],
 ['wiki:Nalanda_mahavihara','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1502/|UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Archaeological Site of Nalanda Mahavihara|heritage'],'Nalanda_mahavihara',end=1200,dateNote='Active c. 427–c. 1200 CE'),
X('samudra-manthan','The churning of the ocean: gods and demons pull the serpent Vasuki for the nectar of immortality',M,'myth-legend',2,-400,['textual (Puranas, epics)','mythological','iconographic'],
 "In a Hindu myth told in the Mahabharata, the Ramayana and the Puranas, the gods (devas) and their rivals the asuras cooperate to churn the cosmic ocean of milk to obtain amrita, the nectar of immortality. They use Mount Mandara as the churning rod and the serpent king Vasuki as the rope, while Vishnu, as the tortoise Kurma, supports the mountain. The churning first releases a deadly poison, halahala, which Shiva drinks, turning his throat blue, and then treasures including the goddess Lakshmi. Vishnu, disguised as the enchantress Mohini, tricks the asuras out of the nectar. The asura Rahu sneaks a sip and is beheaded; his immortal head chases the Sun and Moon, causing eclipses. Drops of nectar falling to earth are linked to the four sites of the Kumbh Mela. A famous 12th-century relief at Angkor Wat depicts the scene.",
 [('Religious meaning','Within Hinduism the story teaches cosmic cooperation and the need to endure poison before reward, and explains rituals and festivals including the Kumbh Mela, one of the largest gatherings of people on Earth.'),
  ('Eclipse explanation','The Rahu episode is a mythic explanation of eclipses; in Indian astronomy Rahu and Ketu became the names of the lunar nodes, the points where eclipses occur.'),
  ('Precession reading','In Hamlet\'s Mill (1969), Giorgio de Santillana and Hertha von Dechend argued that churning and mill myths worldwide encode knowledge of the precession of the equinoxes; most scholars of myth and astronomy consider this unproven (speculative).'),
  ('Elixir pattern','Comparativists note parallels with other drinks of immortality, such as Greek ambrosia, Norse mead and the Vedic soma, debated as shared Indo-European inheritance or independent invention.')],
 ['wiki:Samudra_manthan','wiki:Kumbh_Mela',"wiki:Hamlet's_Mill"],'Samudra_manthan',dateNote='Texts c. 400 BCE–1000 CE; mythic time'),
]
